1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,080
And then they're trying to figure out a way

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:04,880
to outmaneuver that guy.

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:07,880
Even though, again, given very egalitarian premises,

4
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:09,880
maybe he may be actually the socialist, right?

5
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:12,600
Maybe you have to actually say, hmm,

6
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:16,080
it's better to have inequality without slavery

7
00:00:16,080 --> 00:00:18,080
than slavery without inequality.

8
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:19,720
Maybe you need to go and say that.

9
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:21,960
All right.

10
00:00:23,040 --> 00:00:25,000
All right, everyone happy?

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:26,760
Everyone staying awake?

12
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:29,840
All right, so we're all, gotta stay awake, yeah.

13
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,000
So, Bobby Rowe is the best man at my wedding.

14
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,280
Whenever he gives a late talk,

15
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:37,080
he begins by actually making people cheer and shout things.

16
00:00:37,080 --> 00:00:38,880
I'm not quite ready to go there yet.

17
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:42,200
But, you know, I make people applaud for different things.

18
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,280
I used to divide people up into different factions.

19
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:46,200
All right, anyway.

20
00:00:47,960 --> 00:00:51,440
We'll just continue trying to be excited.

21
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:52,680
Try to stay awake.

22
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:54,440
Okay, next, all right.

23
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,080
So, Errod De Broglie contingent claims market.

24
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:58,760
So, I did just make fun of Gerard De Broglie.

25
00:00:58,760 --> 00:00:59,880
Now, here's an idea that he had

26
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:02,160
that I think is actually more interesting.

27
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:06,040
Now, generally, it starts out seeming pretty general,

28
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:08,440
good for any number of agents, any number of goods.

29
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:09,440
All right.

30
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:11,080
But then Errod De Broglie said, wait a second,

31
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:14,600
this is actually way more general than it seems at first.

32
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:15,440
Why?

33
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:16,280
Well, he said, first of all,

34
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:18,600
our theorem can actually be extended immediately

35
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:20,400
to intertemporal markets.

36
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:21,240
Why?

37
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:24,600
So, just think of good trading at time t

38
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:25,840
as being a different good

39
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:28,560
than good k trading at time t plus y.

40
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:30,080
And then basically, we say,

41
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,840
well, instead of having k physical goods,

42
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:37,120
we can have k times t intertemporal goods.

43
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,520
All right, and then we just go and redefine k.

44
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,320
I'll explain what it is, I'll take new k

45
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:47,600
is gonna be equal to old k times t, the number of periods.

46
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:49,800
And now we've got a model that actually allows

47
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,080
for an arbitrary amount of intertemporal trade.

48
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:56,280
So, now I could go and trade bananas in 2019

49
00:01:56,280 --> 00:01:59,560
for driverless cars in 2037.

50
00:01:59,560 --> 00:02:01,720
And we could go and write a contract about that.

51
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:02,960
We can exchange that,

52
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:05,120
and that could be a perfectly competitive market

53
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:07,400
where we go and exchange pieces of paper

54
00:02:07,400 --> 00:02:09,200
for goods that don't even exist yet.

55
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:12,120
That would be possible.

56
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:13,880
All right, so instead of trading current goods,

57
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:15,040
instead of it being a bargain market

58
00:02:15,040 --> 00:02:17,640
where I hand you bananas now for cars now,

59
00:02:17,640 --> 00:02:20,840
I'm handing pieces of paper which are just promissory notes.

60
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,160
And we can have a giant market in these promissory notes

61
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:24,680
where every conceivable promissory note

62
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,560
can trade for every other conceivable promissory note.

63
00:02:27,560 --> 00:02:29,600
All right, so.

64
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:32,520
And then, they said also general equilibrium theory

65
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:34,680
can handle an arbitrary amount of uncertainty.

66
00:02:34,680 --> 00:02:39,000
So imagine we've got k physical goods, t time periods,

67
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:42,640
and then say m possible states of the world.

68
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:45,800
Then we can just go and say, well, let's multiply them out.

69
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:48,720
So k physical goods, t time periods, m goods,

70
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:53,720
so that'll be k times t times m goods,

71
00:02:53,720 --> 00:02:56,480
which we can exchange as pieces of paper.

72
00:02:56,480 --> 00:02:58,880
So this is a piece of paper that is good

73
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:01,680
for one mouse in the year 2037

74
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:05,440
if the name Brian is more popular than the name Ryan.

75
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:08,040
And that could be a note.

76
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:11,080
And we could go and define an unlimited state,

77
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:12,600
a list of states of affairs,

78
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,320
which might or might not happen.

79
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:15,640
And if the state of affairs doesn't happen,

80
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,720
then the promissory note is worthless.

81
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:21,040
And if it does happen, then it's worth its face value.

82
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:22,340
And we can do it that way.

83
00:03:22,340 --> 00:03:25,740
Okay, so just think of good k,

84
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:28,380
or good k if x happens to be a different good

85
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:30,620
than good k if x doesn't happen.

86
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:32,860
So again, more realistically,

87
00:03:32,860 --> 00:03:36,540
it could be like an orange if the average temperature

88
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:38,380
in Florida is over 80 degrees,

89
00:03:38,380 --> 00:03:40,660
and an orange if the average temperature in Florida

90
00:03:40,660 --> 00:03:43,800
is less than or equal to 80 degrees.

91
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:45,780
And then we could go and have two different contracts

92
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:48,500
for each of these different states of the world.

93
00:03:48,500 --> 00:03:51,540
So again, imagine the incredible complexity

94
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:52,900
of these markets.

95
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:54,020
So it's not just every good,

96
00:03:54,020 --> 00:03:55,580
it's every good at every time period

97
00:03:55,580 --> 00:03:58,460
under every possible imaginable condition.

98
00:03:58,460 --> 00:04:01,020
And we can all shoehorn it into an existing model.

99
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:04,380
Okay, right?

100
00:04:04,380 --> 00:04:05,780
Now intuitively, you can actually imagine

101
00:04:05,780 --> 00:04:08,340
that I take a piece of paper that is good for,

102
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:11,820
say, like one lecture from me in a week.

103
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:15,020
And then I could go and I could draw a bunch of lines on it,

104
00:04:15,020 --> 00:04:16,940
and I could cut it up into

105
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:18,340
a thousand different pieces of paper,

106
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:21,300
good under this condition, good under that condition.

107
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:24,580
And as long as I've actually partitioned it into

108
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:26,740
all logical possibilities,

109
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:31,340
so I divided it up into a set of mutually exclusive,

110
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:33,980
jointly exhaustive conditions.

111
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:35,740
So if you have all the pieces of paper,

112
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:37,860
then you definitely get a free lecture from me.

113
00:04:37,860 --> 00:04:39,420
Or if not a free lecture, you get a lecture from me

114
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:40,740
if you're the owner of it.

115
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:44,260
Then we could go and imagine a market in all these goods.

116
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:46,620
Like every piece of paper can be sold separately.

117
00:04:46,620 --> 00:04:48,340
And then if you want a guarantee,

118
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:50,100
you just buy up all the pieces.

119
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:51,300
If you want a semi-guarantee,

120
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:54,180
then you buy up a segment of the pieces.

121
00:04:55,620 --> 00:04:56,460
Or not.

122
00:04:57,660 --> 00:04:58,500
Okay.

123
00:04:59,420 --> 00:05:00,700
All right.

124
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:02,780
Now, so anyways, in all these cases,

125
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:05,060
this is taking advantage of the fact

126
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:07,060
that we can construct a new problem

127
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:09,820
which is isomorphic to another one.

128
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:12,180
Right, which when you really think about it

129
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:14,980
is actually logically the same as another problem.

130
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:17,660
All right.

131
00:05:17,660 --> 00:05:20,980
Yeah, so, you know, like lots of problems.

132
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:23,140
So again, if you were doing price controls

133
00:05:23,140 --> 00:05:25,020
and you started off with rent control,

134
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:26,900
and then someone says, what about labor?

135
00:05:26,900 --> 00:05:28,980
And you just go, here, I'll prove it for labor too.

136
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:31,900
And you just erase the word rent and rent control

137
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:36,020
and you replace it with the word wage maximum.

138
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:39,420
And then the diagram's still good.

139
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:42,020
Right, so that would be a case in my smart problem

140
00:05:42,020 --> 00:05:45,300
where we start off like a price dealing for asparagus.

141
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:46,780
Well, what if it was coal?

142
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:48,700
Erase coal, write down coal.

143
00:05:48,700 --> 00:05:50,860
Now it's the diagram for coal, ta-da!

144
00:05:50,860 --> 00:05:52,220
All right, isn't that amazing?

145
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:56,220
All right, same thing here with the Arrow and Dubrow Realize.

146
00:05:56,220 --> 00:05:59,140
You can take their general equilibrium model

147
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:00,340
and you can just go and say,

148
00:06:00,340 --> 00:06:02,300
you know when we were talking about physical goods?

149
00:06:02,300 --> 00:06:04,740
They could be notional goods.

150
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:06,300
Any number of notional goods

151
00:06:06,300 --> 00:06:10,060
under any conceivable situation that you can imagine.

152
00:06:10,060 --> 00:06:11,900
All logical possibilities.

153
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:14,420
All through the future history of the universe.

154
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:16,380
And our model applies.

155
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:17,220
All right.

156
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:20,220
Now you may think, eh, it's still kind of like over problem,

157
00:06:20,220 --> 00:06:24,220
but there have actually been some real concrete achievements

158
00:06:24,220 --> 00:06:26,900
that I think have been inspired by Arrow and Dubrow.

159
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:30,900
And the most notable one in my view is betting markets.

160
00:06:34,340 --> 00:06:38,180
Right now, if you are a very narrowly minded person,

161
00:06:38,180 --> 00:06:39,700
as I'm sure none of you are,

162
00:06:39,700 --> 00:06:43,340
I say betting and you think Atlantic City, right?

163
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:45,020
Right?

164
00:06:45,020 --> 00:06:47,940
But, if you think about it a bit more,

165
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:51,540
you say, well really, aren't all financial markets

166
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:52,820
really betting markets too?

167
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:55,740
Aren't people betting on prices going up or down?

168
00:06:55,740 --> 00:06:57,060
And you say, so really betting markets

169
00:06:57,060 --> 00:06:58,660
are not just Atlantic City, it's all the stuff

170
00:06:58,660 --> 00:07:01,180
and we're not insurance, it's not really kind of betting.

171
00:07:01,180 --> 00:07:02,940
And when you start thinking about it more,

172
00:07:02,940 --> 00:07:05,500
it's saying, well gee, if that's all true,

173
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:09,980
couldn't we have a betting market about any event at all?

174
00:07:09,980 --> 00:07:12,100
That anyone wonders about?

175
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:14,100
Couldn't we have a betting market on whether

176
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:16,460
say Donald Trump will still be the president

177
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:17,620
at the end of his term?

178
00:07:18,580 --> 00:07:19,700
We can have that market.

179
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:22,900
And now, partly thanks to these ideas we do,

180
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:24,340
there are online betting markets

181
00:07:24,340 --> 00:07:26,440
for all kinds of weird things.

182
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:27,680
There's online betting markets,

183
00:07:27,680 --> 00:07:29,000
not just for political things,

184
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:31,140
like which party would retain control of Congress.

185
00:07:31,140 --> 00:07:34,160
There are online betting markets for unemployment rates,

186
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:35,440
for inflation rates,

187
00:07:35,440 --> 00:07:38,220
online betting markets for revolutions, right?

188
00:07:38,220 --> 00:07:41,380
And then there was a very controversial online market

189
00:07:41,380 --> 00:07:43,180
that the great Robin Hanson,

190
00:07:43,180 --> 00:07:44,260
as I mentioned a few times already,

191
00:07:44,260 --> 00:07:47,340
but he always deserves all the bliss that I can give him.

192
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,180
So there's a very controversial betting market

193
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:51,780
that Robin was personally very closely involved in.

194
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:55,700
So gee, you guys are so young,

195
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:58,940
you used this probably before most of your times.

196
00:07:58,940 --> 00:07:59,940
All right, scary.

197
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:02,840
But perhaps not as scary as the idea itself.

198
00:08:02,840 --> 00:08:12,840
And the idea itself was terrorism betting markets.

199
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:18,020
Can I draw like a scholar or something?

200
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:24,060
Spooky, all right.

201
00:08:24,060 --> 00:08:24,900
All right.

202
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:26,380
All right.

203
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:30,600
So about 15 years ago,

204
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:33,100
it's about 15 years ago now,

205
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:36,340
DARPA, which is the Research Wing of the Defense Department

206
00:08:36,340 --> 00:08:39,480
funded a set of internal betting markets

207
00:08:39,480 --> 00:08:44,000
for predicting grotesque and horrifying events.

208
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:46,060
And there was a whole team of researchers

209
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:47,380
that were involved in this.

210
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:48,940
Robin Hanson was one of them.

211
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:52,280
And then word leaked out that this was occurring.

212
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:54,060
And there was a Senator and a Congressman

213
00:08:54,060 --> 00:08:57,140
and a representative immediately at a press conference

214
00:08:57,140 --> 00:08:58,380
denouncing this ghoulish

215
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:01,700
and horrible terrorism betting markets.

216
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:02,540
And if you ever believe

217
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:04,140
that there is a political status quo bias

218
00:09:04,140 --> 00:09:06,420
and it's hard to change anything in policy,

219
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:07,820
that's not what happened here.

220
00:09:07,820 --> 00:09:10,140
Within, the program was immediately killed

221
00:09:10,140 --> 00:09:11,220
and within two days,

222
00:09:11,220 --> 00:09:13,500
all the main people involved had been fired

223
00:09:13,500 --> 00:09:14,820
or had to resign.

224
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:15,660
All right.

225
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,660
Now, most of the researchers involved was read for cover.

226
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:20,620
Robin was the one guy who kept answering his phone

227
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:22,140
and saying, yes, I would love to defend

228
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:24,420
so-called terrorism betting markets.

229
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:25,940
Now, Robin to this day insists

230
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:28,780
it wasn't really terrorism betting markets.

231
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:30,380
Right now, here is his story.

232
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:32,420
You can tell me if this makes any sense to you.

233
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:34,500
And I don't think it would make sense to many people.

234
00:09:34,500 --> 00:09:37,060
Says, look, it wasn't really betting on terrorism

235
00:09:37,060 --> 00:09:40,620
because you couldn't bet on any specific terrorist attack.

236
00:09:40,620 --> 00:09:42,020
You can only bet on the total number

237
00:09:42,020 --> 00:09:43,520
of terrorism deaths per year.

238
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:46,040
Now, to a normal person,

239
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:47,380
does that mean you're not really betting

240
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:48,620
on terrorism anymore?

241
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:51,900
No.

242
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:53,220
Like this is just a case of Robin

243
00:09:53,220 --> 00:09:56,220
not understanding the emotions that are normal for a person.

244
00:09:56,220 --> 00:09:59,620
Which of course is what makes him so creative

245
00:09:59,620 --> 00:10:00,780
is just like,

246
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:04,820
like if you even knew the emotions, that might stop it.

247
00:10:04,820 --> 00:10:06,900
But since he's unaware of these emotions,

248
00:10:06,900 --> 00:10:07,740
then he just does it.

249
00:10:07,740 --> 00:10:08,740
He's like, what did I say?

250
00:10:08,740 --> 00:10:09,940
What did I do?

251
00:10:09,940 --> 00:10:13,020
But anyway, so terrorism betting markets.

252
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:14,980
Again, the idea here is why don't we go

253
00:10:14,980 --> 00:10:17,460
and have a betting market where people go

254
00:10:17,460 --> 00:10:20,180
and actually place bets on the total number

255
00:10:20,180 --> 00:10:21,020
of terrorism deaths.

256
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:22,340
The easiest way to imagine it

257
00:10:22,340 --> 00:10:25,900
is something like suppose that there is a security

258
00:10:25,900 --> 00:10:28,180
which pays a dollar if the total number

259
00:10:28,180 --> 00:10:30,460
of terrorism deaths is over 2,000

260
00:10:30,460 --> 00:10:31,620
and another one pays a dollar

261
00:10:31,620 --> 00:10:33,460
if it is less than or equal to 2,000.

262
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:35,240
And then we can go and compare the prices

263
00:10:35,240 --> 00:10:36,500
of these two things and this will give us

264
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:37,340
an idea about those.

265
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:38,780
Or we could have many more,

266
00:10:38,780 --> 00:10:41,080
so we could have a whole bunch of different bins

267
00:10:41,080 --> 00:10:42,780
